<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">12 June 1987 · Brandenburg Gate, West Berlin</p><h1 class="sp-title">Reagan: Tear Down This Wall</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Visiting West Berlin for the city's 750th anniversary, President Ronald Reagan spoke in front of the Brandenburg Gate, which stood just beyond the Berlin Wall. Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms had raised hopes of change in the Soviet bloc, and against the objections of some of his advisers Reagan challenged the Soviet leader directly.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Mr. Gorbachev, open this gate! Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Remarks at the Brandenburg Gate, West Berlin, 12 June 1987.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://www.reaganlibrary.gov/archives/speech/remarks-east-west-relations-brandenburg-gate-west-berlin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Ronald Reagan Presidential Library</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The speech drew little attention at the time. Two years later, as revolutions swept Eastern Europe, East Germany opened the Berlin Wall on 9 November 1989; Germany was reunited in October 1990, and the Soviet Union itself dissolved at the end of 1991.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">Revolutions of 1989</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">Fall of the Berlin Wall</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">Dissolution of the Soviet Union</a></div></div></section>
